{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent level bias: forecasts are generally too low by ~+1.0 to +2.5 €/t for the first ~1–20 days when the market is in an upswing; apply an upward intercept shift that is strongest at short horizons and tapers with horizon.",
    "Underreaction to upward momentum: after a rising 1–2 week trend, the path is too flat/low (misses the step-up). Add a momentum term so that positive recent returns lift the entire near-term trajectory.",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: at longer horizons (~day 20–30) the model often mean-reverts too aggressively and can drop below realized prices (notably after high prints). Damp mean reversion beyond ~2–3 weeks (pull forecasts toward the recent average more slowly).",
    "Peak/trough timing (lag): the model tends to place turning points a few days early/late; smooth the forecast path and shift inflection points slightly forward when recent trend is strong (i.e., don’t call reversals too early).",
    "Volatility underestimation around rallies: realized swings during bullish phases are larger than forecast; widen the forecast range or increase day-to-day slope when recent realized volatility is elevated."
  ]
}
